
### Network Geography

Personal Area Network (PAN): Smallest type of wired or wireless network which usually covers a distance of about 10 feet or less
	Ex. Bluetooth and USB
		Bluetooth transmitting music to your car sound system is an example of a PAN. Another is connecting a USB drive to a computer.

Local Area Network (LAN): Connects components in a limited distance, up to about 300 feet or 100 meters
	Ex. Home, floor of an office building, classroom

Campus Area Network (CAN): Connects multiple LANs that are spread across numerous buildings in a certain area
	Can cover several miles and many buildings
	Ex. College campuses may utilize a CAN to link multiple LANs across multiple buildings
	Also used by military bases or business parks

Metropolitan Area Network (MAN): Connects locations that are scattered across the entire city
	Larger than a CAN, but smaller than a WAN
	Ex. Separate campuses of one college may connect their CANs/LANs via a MAN

Wide Area Network (WAN): Connects geographically disparate internal networks
	Ex. Leased lines, virtual private networks
	Covers a very large geographic area, Ex. Country, or even the world
	The Internet is a WAN, and the largest WAN at that
	WAN does not always need to be public like the Internet

Network geography is about the distance involved

Networks consist of either Wi-Fi or Ethernet
Wi-Fi utilizes the IEEE 802.11 Standard
Ethernet utilizes the IEEE 802.3 Standard

### Wired Network Topology

Network Topology: Refers to the arrangements of different elements like links, nodes, clients, and servers that make up a computer network

Topologies are documented in diagrams using either Physical Topology or Logical Topology

Physical Topology: Used to show how the network devices and components are physically cabled and connected together and where they're located in the real world environment

Logical Topology: Talks about how the traffic is actually going to flow in the network
	Logical diagram doesn't show the actual physical location of the devices or use a floor plan
	Demonstrates the way the data flows between the devices

Topologies:
	Point-to-point
	Ring
	Bus
	Star
	Hub-and-spoke
	Mesh

Point-to-point: Simplest form of topology that involves a direct connection between two devices
	Often used for connecting computers to a peripheral like a scanner or printer

Ring Topology: A network configuration where each device is connected to two other devices, forming a circular data path
	Data travels in one direction around the circle until it reaches its destination
	Prevents data collisions
	If one node fails, it can disrupt the whole network
	Not very common, unless used in Fiber Distributed Data Interface (FDDI)
		Used to conduct data transmissions on fiber optic lines in a local area network
		Operates on a dual ring structure to provide redundancy

Bus Topology: All of the network devices are connected to a single, central cable, called the bus or backbone
	Data can be sent by any device and is available to every other device on the bus, but only the intended recipient can receive data
	Easy to install, less cable
	Entire network can be disabled if the bus fails
	More traffic can cause data collisions
	Not common, older tech

Star Topology: One of the most common topologies, used often in homes
	Each node connects back to a central connection point, like a switch
	Very robust, failure of one link does not disrupt others
	If central connection point fails, the whole network can be disrupted

Hub-and-spoke Topology: Variation of the Star Topology where the central node (hub) is connected to multiple nodes (spokes)
	Spokes are not directly connected, must transmit data to a hub which then relays it
	Commonly used in airlines
	For example, multiple offices in a city may transmit data to a nearby hub. Said hub will then transmit to another distant hub, where it's then distributed to all of the linked devices on that one

Mesh Topology: Features a point-to-point connection between every single device on the network to create a robust and redundant network
	Full-mesh: Every node is connected to every other node in the network. Can be expensive and difficult to install in larger environments, however.
		x = n(n-1)/2 where *n= number of nodes*
	Partial-mesh: Some nodes are organized in a full mesh scheme, while others are only connected to one or two devices in the network
		More important nodes may be connected to all nodes, while less critical nodes may only be connected to crucial ones

Summary
Point-to-point: Simple, reliable for small scale connections
Ring and Bus: Simple, vulnerable to single points of failure
Star and Hub-and-spoke: Centralized, however rely heavily on the hub/central point
Mesh: Robustness, redundancy, but complex and costly for full mesh. Partial mesh gives up some redundancy

### Data Center Topology

Data Center: Any facility used by businesses to organize, process, store, and disseminate large amounts of data
	Can vary widely in size, from 150 square feet to 1.5 million square feet like the Utah Data Center

Data centers utilize the following topologies:
	Three-Tiered Hierarchy
	Collapsed Core
	Spine and Leaf Architecture
	Traffic Flows

Three Tiered Hierarchy: Consists of three layers:
	Core: Backbone of network, merges geographically separated networks back into one cohesive unit. Have at least two routers that operate in a redundant configuration.
	Distribution/Aggregation: Under the core layer, implements access lists and filters. Defines the policies for the network. Switches will be found here routing to different subnets and VLANs
	Access/Edge: Beneath the distribution layer, connects all endpoint devices. Switches found here, converts packets to frames and ensures they're delivered to correct endpoints
	Provides redundancy and reliability
	One device can be down while the rest of the network functions as normal

Collapsed Core: Network architecture where the core and distribution layers are merged into a single layer, giving a two-tiered system
	Functions of the distribution and core layers are combined, simplifying architecture
	Found in small or medium sized data centers where three tiered is not needed
	Is not suitable for larger and more complex networks

Spine and Leaf Architecture: Alternative type of network architecture that focuses on the communication within the datacenter itself
	Spine: Connects switches, creating a full mesh topology
	Leaf: Consists of all access switches, aggregates traffic from different servers and connects into the spine
	Faster speeds, lower latency than three-tiered
	Works well with Software Defined Networking
	Can be used in combination with three-tiered
		All the servers connect to leaf layers, which connect to the spine, which connects to the core layer of the three-tiered model

Traffic Flows
	North-South: Traffic enters or leaves the data center from a system residing outside of the data center
		Northbound Traffic: Leaving datacenter
		Southbound Traffic: Entering datacenter
	East-West: Refers to data flow within a data center
